From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Prepare the frame where it is encoded

Resizing a picture and turning it into the colours an encoder wants is done
on every frame, and it was always done in software. That was reasonable while
the encoding was too: the frame was in the processor's memory anyway. It is
not reasonable once the encoding happens elsewhere, because the frame is then
carried out of the graphics after being resized only to be carried back in to
be encoded, and both journeys are paid thirty times a second.

Where the encoder is the one built into the processor's graphics, that part
can resize and convert as well, so it does. The frame goes in once and comes
out encoded. The capabilities between the two now say the frame stays in the
graphics' own memory, without which the two would agree on holding it in the
processor's memory instead and the journeys would remain.

Everywhere else the software elements are kept. Handing a frame to one device
to prepare it for a different one is not a saving, and the encoder on the
card has memory of its own to be carried to regardless.

Nothing about what is sent changes. What changes is how much of the processor
is spent not sending it.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
